1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,688
If the electricity that runs a heat pump comes from a gas or coal power
plant, is the heat pump really any cleaner than the gas furnace it replaced?

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,320
It is a fair question, so
let's put it to the studies.

3
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:28,413
The doubters are easy to find: one letter to the Boston Globe warned that "the majority of electricity in Massachusetts is sourced from gas- and
coal-fired power plants", and the American Gas Association argues that a gas home can have a smaller carbon footprint than a heat pump home.

4
00:00:28,433 --> 00:00:39,593
The channel Technology Connections answers that even with a gas-fired power plant behind it, "a
heat pump can move more heat energy into a living space than the natural gas itself contains".

5
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,128
So which is it, in real
homes, on real grids?

6
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:48,741
Start in Britain, with households that
replaced a gas boiler with a heat pump.

7
00:00:49,067 --> 00:01:05,891
A working paper turned the change in those households' energy use into emissions and found, in today's terms, a cut of 1.16
tonnes of CO2 equivalent per household per year: 36 percent of the footprint their energy use had before the heat pump.

8
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:19,232
The authors also assume the electricity grid keeps getting cleaner, and on that assumption project
a 68 percent cut across an assumed 20-year working life: a lifetime projection, not today's figure.

9
00:01:19,233 --> 00:01:24,345
But not every grid is Britain's. What
happens where coal still runs the show?

10
00:01:24,367 --> 00:01:33,295
A report from RMI compared electric heating with gas in American cities on the
grid of the time, and found it cut carbon in Oakland, Houston and Providence.

11
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:42,984
But in Chicago, where coal is the power that answers extra demand most of
the year, electrifying heat increased carbon emissions in the short term.

12
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:51,052
RMI expected that to swing in favour of electrification,
in Chicago and nationally, as coal plants keep retiring.

13
00:01:52,067 --> 00:02:07,547
The Canadian Climate Institute, citing the International Energy Agency and a consulting firm, says that even on relatively
emissions-intensive electricity, heat pumps emit 20 to 30 percent less carbon than gas furnaces, because they are so efficient.

14
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:16,531
That is a summary of other work, not a measurement on any one grid,
so it sits beside RMI's Chicago result rather than overturning it.

15
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:20,421
So how many American homes sit
on the wrong side of that line?

16
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:38,769
One national model found switching to a heat pump would raise CO2 emissions in just 2.1 percent of the US housing stock, a share of all
houses rather than of gas homes alone, but that estimate assumes grid CO2 emissions fall 45 percent over the heat pump's 15-year life.

17
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:56,560
A second national study, from US national laboratories, modelled every home switching to a heat pump at once, not only gas-heated ones, and
found average greenhouse gas savings positive in every state, in every future grid scenario it modelled, even the most conservative one.

18
00:02:56,867 --> 00:03:06,779
Notice what that measures: an average for each state on grids the authors project
into the future, not today's grid, and not a count of the homes that come out behind.

19
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:09,748
The cleaner the grid, the bigger the gain.

20
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:24,863
In a Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory study of home retrofits, the group of projects built around electrification, with
solar panels, sat mostly in states with low-carbon electricity, and there switching fuels sharply boosted their carbon savings.

21
00:03:25,167 --> 00:03:32,751
The authors expect similar projects to save less carbon
in states whose electricity is more carbon-intensive.

22
00:03:33,767 --> 00:03:50,183
So does swapping gas for a heat pump cut emissions on today's grid? In the British homes, yes, by about a third of their energy footprint,
and in RMI's words it already cuts carbon on today's grid in all but the most coal-heavy systems, though that was the grid when RMI wrote it.

23
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:53,332
The exception is a grid like Chicago's.

24
00:03:53,633 --> 00:04:05,369
How rare that exception is, the evidence can only estimate: the national American studies
both build in a grid that gets cleaner, so neither is a reading of today's grid alone.

25
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:16,528
If carbon is why you are looking at a heat pump, the question to put to your power company is not
whether it burns anything, but what it burns when demand rises, and whether that is changing.

26
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:20,433
Know that, and you know the part of
the answer these studies turn on.
